# Design: serial console receive path (draft 1)
Author: Ana. Reviewers: (two peers). Status: draft.

## Context
The kernel of OS302 prints to the serial port but cannot read from it yet.
We want a shell, so keyboard input over the serial line must reach a task.

## Goals
G1: Every byte typed reaches the console task in order.
G2: The receive path is fast and robust.
G3: A paste of a long line does not crash the kernel.

## Non-goals
Flow control, other UARTs, etc.

## Design
The receive interrupt pushes each byte into a ring; the console task pops bytes.
The ring is a simple array with head and tail indices.

## Interfaces
bool rx_pop(uint8_t& b) returns false when nothing is waiting.
bool rx_wait(int timeout) waits until a byte is available or the timeout expires.
Ring size 8.

## Failure modes
If the ring is full we handle it appropriately.

## Test plan
T1 (G1): type "hello" in QEMU, the shell echoes "hello".
T2 (G2): run the shell for a while and check nothing breaks.
